%% ================================================================ PREFACE
\section*{Introduction}

\initial{M}{odlitwa gdy dziatki spać idą} (‘A prayer when the children go to
sleep’) is an evening hymn in four six-line stanzas by Andrzej Trzecieski the
Younger, set for four voices by Wacław z Szamotuł. Łazarz Andrysowic printed
it in Kraków as a booklet of four leaves. The print is undated. It belongs
with Andrysowic’s other prints of Wacław’s Polish songs, which are placed
between 1550 and 1556.

The title page names no composer. The letters W and S stand after the final
notes of the Tenor, Altus and Bassus, and that monogram is the evidence for
Wacław’s authorship. Michał Wiszniewski described the print in 1844 as
‘4 kart. Z nutami na 4 tony W. S.’ (‘4 leaves. With notes for 4 voices. W. S.’)

The setting is a cantional song. The Tenor carries the hymn tune in breves
and semibreves, one note to each syllable. The other voices move around it
more freely, with melismas on stressed syllables and a clausula at the end of
most lines. The tune also appears on its own, unattributed, in Jan
Seklucjan’s \pl{Pieśni chrześcijańskie dawniejsze i nowe} (Königsberg, 1559).
Whether Wacław wrote it or arranged an older tune is disputed: Mazurkiewicz
takes it for his, Hławiczka for an older song.

No copy of the print is known today. In 1880 Kazimiera Przyborowska published
a facsimile of it in Warsaw, ‘z pierwodruku’ (‘from the first printing’), and every later edition goes
back to that facsimile. The usual modern editions transpose the piece up a
minor third, halve the note values, add a three-flat signature and modernise
the text. This edition gives the music at the pitch and in the note values of
the print, draws the bar lines between the staves only, and keeps the old
forms of the text.

\section*{The song and its use}

The title says what the song is for. Luther’s Small Catechism (1529) ends
with prayers that ‘ein Hausvater sein Gesinde soll lehren’ (‘a head of
household should teach his household’), among them an
evening blessing for bedtime: ‘denn ich befehle mich, meinen Leib und Seele
und alles in deine Hände. Dein heiliger Engel sei mit mir, daß der böse Feind
keine Macht an mir finde.’ (‘for I commend myself, my body and soul and all
things into your hands. Let your holy angel be with me, that the wicked enemy
may find no power over me.’) Polish Lutherans took this over directly. The
second edition of Jan Seklucjan’s \la{Catechismus} (Königsberg, 1549) closes
with a \pl{Nauka dziecinna}, a teaching for children, which tells them how to
begin and end the day:

\begin{quote}
‘Idąc spać mają dać dobrą noc swoim starszym, a niż się układą, mają się
naprzód przeżegnać … [i] te modlitwę mogą przydać: Wszechmogący Ojcze
niebieski, dziękujem Tobie … iżeś nas tego dnia raczył we zdrowiu zachować
i wszelkiej szkody uchować … a przeto my ciała swoje i dusze i ine wszystki
rzeczy w ręce Twoje polecamy, aby Twój święty anioł był przy nas, aby
dyjabeł żadnego prawa do nas nie miał.’
\translation{‘When going to sleep they are to bid their elders good night,
and before they lie down they are first to cross themselves … [and] they may
add this prayer: Almighty heavenly Father, we thank you … that you have
deigned to keep us in health this day and to preserve us from all harm …
and therefore we commend our bodies and souls and all other things into
your hands, that your holy angel may be with us, that the devil may have no
claim on us.’}
\end{quote}

Trzecieski’s third and fourth stanzas put this prayer into verse almost
clause by clause. The holy angel is sent against the enemies of the soul; God
is asked to keep the household ‘przez wszej szkody’ (‘without any harm’) and from
every sudden ‘przygoda’ (‘mishap’); everything the household has is put into his care. The song is
the catechism’s evening prayer, made singable for the family.

Behind both stands Compline, the old night office, which the household
prayer replaced. Its texts are close to Trzecieski’s first stanza. The short
lesson warns that ‘adversarius vester diabolus tamquam leo rugiens circuit’ (‘your adversary the
devil, as a roaring lion, goes about’);
the hymn \la{Te lucis} asks ‘hostemque nostrum comprime’ (‘and hold
back our enemy’); Psalm 90 promises
safety ‘a negotio perambulante in tenebris’ (‘from the thing that walks
about in the dark’); the collect asks God to drive off
‘omnes insidias inimici’ (‘all the snares of the enemy’) and to let his holy angels keep the house in peace.
The parallel is my own reading; I know of no study that makes it.

The hymnals show the song in this domestic use. Zaremba’s Calvinist \pl{Pieśni
chwał boskich} (‘Songs of God’s praises’; Brest, 1558), printed for Mikołaj Radziwiłł, Wacław’s patron,
has the text, in a book that also contains Jakub Sylwiusz’s \pl{Pasterstwo
domowe}, an order for prayer and singing in the household. Seklucjan’s
hymnal of 1559 prints the tune among eight songs headed ‘Idąc spać’ (‘Going to
sleep’). Agnieszka Leszczyńska has suggested that Wacław wrote partly with
young singers in mind, since the Tenor’s plain tune could go to the least
practised voice.

Trzecieski (c.\,1525 – after 1584) was a Kraków humanist and a leader of the
Reformation in Little Poland, a friend of Kochanowski and one of the
translators of the Brest Bible (1563). He knew Wacław well. He wrote verses in
praise of him for the \la{Lamentationes} (1553) and, after the composer’s
death, an elegy, \la{De obitu Venceslai Samotulini} (‘On the death of Wacław
of Szamotuły’).

\section*{Sources}

\begin{sigla}
\siglum{A} \pl{Modlitwa gdy dźyatki śpáć idą.} W Krákowye v Łázárzá
  Andrysowicá. Undated [c.\,1550–1556]. Four leaves, signed A. Title and
  woodcut of two children kneeling before their parents (A1r); Cantus (A1v)
  facing Tenor (A2r); Bassus (A2v) facing Altus (A3r); stanzas 1–3 (A3v);
  stanza 4 and ‘Dokończenye’ (A4r). Clefs C1, C3, C4, F4; cut-C in every voice;
  no signature, no accidentals. No copy known.
\siglum{F} Facsimile of \sig{A}: ‘z Pierwodruku wznowiła Kazimiera Przyborowska.
  Warszawa. 1880 r.’ Warsaw, Biblioteka Narodowa, Podr.SD Mag.F I.6, on
  Polona. A redrawn copy, not a photograph. \textit{Base text.}
\siglum{S} Jan Seklucjan, \pl{Pieśni chrześcijańskie dawniejsze i nowe}
  (Königsberg, 1559), p.\,115: the tune alone.
\end{sigla}

\noindent The print itself is lost, and \sig{F} is a hand-drawn copy of it. \sig{S} has
not yet been collated. Editions collated:

\begin{sigla}
\siglum{PWM} \pl{Już się zmierzcha. Na chór mieszany a cappella}, Polska
  Literatura Chóralna 208 (Kraków: PWM, 1955). Up a minor third; tempo,
  dynamics and breath marks added.
\siglum{K} \pl{Modlitwa, gdy dziatki spać idą}, Towarzystwo Muzyczne im.
  Edwina Kowalika (2015). Stanzas 1–2 underlaid.
\siglum{M} Maia McCormick, \pl{Modlitwa, gdy dziatki spać idą} (2024).
\end{sigla}

\noindent \sig{PWM}, \sig{K} and \sig{M} agree in every note with \sig{F}, apart from the places
listed in the critical notes.

\section*{Editorial method}

\subsection{Pitch, values and barring} As in \sig{A}: final D, no signature, no
reduction of note values, and the print’s cut-C. A bar is one breve, the
tactus of cut-C. Light bar lines run between the staves only, so that none
cuts through a note; a note that runs across one keeps its single value, and
no ties are added. They serve ensemble and rehearsal, and the parts can be
read past them. The
incipit before each staff gives the clef, sign and first note of \sig{A}, and the
range of each voice follows it. The final notes are longae, as in \sig{A}, and are held until all voices have finished; no fermata is added.

\subsection{Clefs and voices} The voice names are those of \sig{A}. Altus and Tenor
use the octave-transposing treble clef. Written ranges: Cantus c′–d″, Altus
d–a′, Tenor d–e′, Bassus G–a.

\subsection{Accidentals} \sig{A} prints none, so every accidental here is editorial.
They stand above the note and apply to that note only. They raise the
cadences where a sixth opens to an octave, in bars 2, 4, 9 and 29. The fifth
such cadence, bar 19, is left as printed. Two signs are optional: a sharp in
bar 4 (Cantus) and a flat in bar 29 (Altus). An accidental in brackets above a note is optional: the note as printed is the edition’s reading, and the bracketed sign an alteration singers may make.

\subsection{Underlay} \sig{A} prints each voice’s text as a block under the staff,
so the underlay is editorial. Three things in \sig{A} guide it. The Tenor’s text
fits its notes exactly, one syllable to a note. Where \sig{A} spaces the words of
the Cantus and Bassus apart, it leaves room for a melisma. The line breaks of
the other voices give only a rough guide. Elsewhere the underlay follows the
rules of this series: no new syllable on a semiminim, except straight after a
dotted minim, or on a fusa; a repeated note takes a new syllable where the
text allows; the last syllable of a line falls on its cadence note. Where
these leave a choice, the words decide. The syllable that carries a line
takes its melisma or its high point (\pl{PO-moc, CZAR-tów, chy-TRO-ści});
a light word such as \pl{o} or \pl{nas} takes one or two notes. The voices
do not always change syllable together. At the two main cadences the Cantus
comes a minim after the Tenor and Bassus: \pl{nas o-bro-} in bars 19–20,
\pl{chy-tro-} in bars 28–29.

\subsection{Text} \sig{A} prints four stanzas and ‘Dokończenye’ (‘The end’), with no Amen. All
four are sung to the same music. Old Polish verse counts syllables, eight to
the line with a break after the fourth, but not stresses, which fall in
different places from stanza to stanza. Stanzas 1 and 2 are underlaid, since
most performances sing one or two, and each has its own underlay where the
other’s would put a melisma on a light syllable: Cantus lines 1, 2, 4 and 6,
Altus line 3, Bassus lines 1 and 3. So the Cantus sings \pl{PA-nie} and
\pl{pie-KIEL-ne} in stanza 2, \pl{WIECZ-ny PO-kój} where stanza 1 has
\pl{CZAR-tów nas}. At the last cadence stanza 1 spreads \pl{swej} over three minims, so that
\pl{chy-tro-} trails the lower voices; stanza 2 gives the run to \pl{wo-}, so
that \pl{-ła-} falls on the leading note. Stanzas 3 and 4 follow the score and take the notes of stanza 1
syllable for syllable. The transcription uses modern spelling and punctuation but keeps
the old sounds and forms: \pl{zmierzka, naszem, strażem, nawięcej, wszytki,
swoję, Jesu, raczysz, wszej, jenż}. The diplomatic text keeps the spelling
and line division of \sig{A}, with ſ, and ē unexpanded.

